    "final_cot": "Image description: The figure is triangle RSP, point T is on the base SP, and point Q is on side RP. The base is divided into ST=4 and TP=10. On the hypotenuse RP, RQ=6, and QP is labeled 2x+1. Side RS and segment QT both have arrow markings in the same direction, indicating QT ∥ RS. The problem asks to find PQ. Reasoning: Because QT ∥ RS, the small triangle QTP is similar to the large triangle RSP. Corresponding sides are proportional: QP/RP = TP/SP. From the figure, SP = ST + TP = 4 + 10 = 14; RP = RQ + QP = 6 + (2x+1) = 2x+7. Set up the proportion: (2x+1)/(2x+7) = 10/14. Simplifying gives 14(2x+1)=10(2x+7), that is, 28x+14=20x+70, so 8x=56, x=7. Therefore PQ = 2x+1 = 2(7)+1 = 15.",
